"""STND.STDC draft 0.3: the C standard, on the custodian's standing directions of the round of 2026-10-24: the
practice specific to C, agnostic of compiler, edition, platform, implementation, and tool and timeless, modeled on
STND.CXX in the form of STND.PY and STND.JL. It applies the provisions of STND.CXX whose outcome holds for C in a
C reading that its Terms define, cites STND.CODE, STND.CONC, STND.DEPS, STND.DOCS, STND.BUILD, and NUMER for what
they hold, and adds only what C makes necessary (GOV.31). Draft 0.3 answers the cold and pair reviews of draft 0.1 and the verification of draft 0.2
(review-records/round-2026-10-24/c/dispositions.md) and is rehearsed on STND.CXX 1.1 and STND.BUILD."""
from content3 import P, NORMATIVE, TERMS_LEAD

DATE = "2026-10-24"

PURPOSE = """This standard holds the coding practice that is specific to C. It adds to STND.CODE, STND.DOCS, and STND.CONC only what the language makes necessary. It applies to C source each provision of STND.CXX whose outcome holds for C, in a C reading that its Terms define, in place of restating it (STND.STDC.1 and STND.STDC.2), records each lowering of the edition of a translation unit (STND.STDC.3), and asks a test of each conditional feature that a unit uses (STND.STDC.4). It states how code without destructors releases what it owns, passes sequences with their extent, and keeps each access within its object and the lifetime of that object (STND.STDC.5 to STND.STDC.10). It excludes the classes of undefined behavior that optimizers exploit and keeps the correctness of code independent of options that define them (STND.STDC.11 to STND.STDC.16), and covers integer conversions and the types of sizes (STND.STDC.17 and STND.STDC.18), strings and buffers (STND.STDC.19 to STND.STDC.22), the preprocessor (STND.STDC.23, STND.STDC.24, and STND.STDC.25), restrict-qualified pointers and variable-length arrays (STND.STDC.26 and STND.STDC.27), stream errors, failures, errno, and ignored results (STND.STDC.28 to STND.STDC.32), signal handlers and bit-fields shared between threads (STND.STDC.33, STND.STDC.34, and STND.STDC.35), data that crosses to other languages (STND.STDC.36), and the checks of C code (STND.STDC.37 and STND.STDC.38). Through STND.STDC.1 and STND.STDC.2, the high-assurance provisions of STND.CXX apply to C: conformance to the high-assurance coding standard that a decision record of the work adopts, claims of conformance limited to what was checked, an edition that the standard covers and the toolchain is validated for, and recorded deviations.

STND.STDC.2 instantiates PRIME.4 and PRIME.2, and is binding at every class. Every other provision is a default (GOV.19), which a project replaces by a recorded decision with an alternative that meets the same higher-level outcome (REPRO.7). The standard is compiler, edition, platform, and tool agnostic: its provisions name the constructs of C and no edition, compiler, library to depend on, platform, or tool, and the repository configuration or a project instrument chooses them (GOV.18). It sets no edition for C; the repository configuration states the edition of each translation unit. The build matter that it holds is that of two applied provisions, the C reading of STND.CXX.4, which states the edition, disables extensions, and states the options that change what a unit means, and the C reading of STND.CXX.25, which records a set of compiler warnings and fails a change on each; the second sentence of STND.STDC.16 adds a run of the tests in a build named apart as STND.BUILD.13 provides. The rules of concurrency that hold for every language are in STND.CONC, which applies to C by its own scope and which this standard cites and does not restate."""

TERMS = TERMS_LEAD + """ Repository configuration and Static check have the meanings that STND.CODE gives them. Translation unit, Toolchain matrix, and Owned resource have the meanings that STND.CXX gives them, and Edition in force the meaning that STND.CXX gives it in its C reading. Concurrent task, Shared mutable object, and Protection have the meanings that STND.CONC gives them; a signal handler is a concurrent task. Checking build has the meaning that STND.BUILD gives it.

| Term | Definition |
| --- | --- |
| C source | Source of the work in C: each file that a compiler of the work translates as C, and each header that such a file includes, other than a header of the implementation or of a dependency, and other than external code that the work incorporates and keeps apart as STND.DEPS.11 recommends. A header that C and C++ translation units both include is C source and C++ source. |
| Foreign interface | Of C source: an interface through which C source calls, or is called by, code written in another language; a callback or a thread that a runtime of another language starts in C source; or a structured block of a compiler directive for parallel execution. |
| C reading | Of a provision or a term of STND.CXX, at the revision of STND.CXX in force: that provision or term applied to C source, with C, the ISO C standard, the C standard library, and an implementation of the C standard library in place of C++, the ISO C++ standard, the C++ standard library, and a standard library implementation; with each term that this standard defines in place of the term of STND.CXX of the same name; with each other term of STND.CXX, and each provision of STND.CXX that it cites, taken in the same reading; and with the change that the row of that provision in the table of STND.STDC.1 or STND.STDC.2 gives. |
| Conditional feature | A feature of an edition of the ISO C standard that the edition lets an implementation omit, and whose presence or absence the implementation reports by a predefined macro that the edition names. |
| Undefined behavior | Behavior for which the ISO C standard, at the edition in force, imposes no requirements. |
| Signal-safe function | A function that the ISO C standard, or the documentation of the platform on which the code runs, states as one that a signal handler is able to call. |
| Untrusted input | Data that reaches the work from outside its own code, other than data from a source that the threat model of the project records as trusted. |"""

NOTES = """The standard is agnostic of compiler, edition, platform, implementation, and tool, and cites the provision that holds an outcome in place of restating it (GOV.31). It is modeled on STND.CXX, in the form of STND.PY and STND.JL, and its binding provision gives C the responses to a listed failure that STND.PY.22 and STND.JL.15 give Python and Julia. Each provision states an outcome that a tool or a reviewer decides, and names constructs of C, which GOV.18 admits at LVL2; the Notes name a compiler, a library function, or a coding standard only to show a hazard. The source is the research note research/c-notes/c1-c-language.md (cited as c1), and a fact that c1 does not support is marked unverified.

C reading. Much of STND.CXX holds for C, because the two languages share a model of compilation, the kinds of their undefined behavior, and the failures at the boundary of a library. STND.STDC.1 and STND.STDC.2 apply those provisions to C source in their C reading, so that each keeps one text and its mode, and this standard holds only what C adds (GOV.31). Each row of STND.STDC.1 carries the class from which it applies, the onset that the Notes of STND.CXX give its provision, and a project replaces one row without the others. The reading follows the revision of STND.CXX in force; where a provision of STND.CXX cites STND.CONC, the cited provision applies to C by its own scope. A header that C and C++ units both include meets both readings where it keeps to what the two languages share; the linkage of a constant object and of an inline function differs between them (unverified).

Provisions of STND.CXX that apply. STND.CXX.4 states the edition, disables extensions, and states the options that change what a unit means; the default dialect of one compiler enables extensions that change the meaning of the base standard (c1, practice 1). Its row adds the options that define a behavior that the language leaves undefined, which its third sentence does not name. STND.BUILD.11 and STND.BUILD.12 are the general rules for settings and warnings in every language, and the rows of STND.CXX.4 and STND.CXX.25 hold the C items. The edition stated is one of the ISO C standard, since a compiler's default is a dialect and its names for editions change (c1, Editions). STND.CXX.7 holds with its row: a register is reached through a volatile-qualified object (c1, What the corpus already holds), and a type alias in C names the same type, so a distinct type for a pointer into device memory is a pointer to a distinct type or a wrapping structure. STND.CXX.9 holds because results of the C library also depend on behavior that the ISO C standard leaves to the implementation; c1 lists STND.CXX.8 to STND.CXX.10 as not carrying, and the examples of such behavior, the sequence of the pseudo-random generator, the order of equal elements after a sort, and the accuracy of mathematical functions, are unverified. STND.CXX.12 is met in C by static assertions; c1 lists it both as not carrying and, in its section on the preprocessor, as carrying, and its text names no C++ facility. STND.CXX.15 gives the ownership contract that C needs most, and STND.STDC.6 adds the function that releases what a declaration returns. STND.CXX.16 keeps every response but a C++ exception, and its row adds that a report through errno alone is not a return of the condition. STND.CXX.17 is met by the attribute that an edition provides for a result not to be discarded, or by a compiler's equivalent (c1, Error reporting and ignored returns). STND.CXX.18 holds as written: NDEBUG removes an assertion, and an option that assumes finite values removes a test for non-finite values. NDEBUG set by an option of the compiler is an execution setting, so a check of untrusted input written as an assertion that it removes, the case of STND.PY.25 and STND.JL.13, is a check that STND.CODE.6 forbids in every language and that STND.CXX.18 cites, and this standard adds no provision for it. STND.CXX.23 gives the C type for an object that a signal handler shares, beside a lock-free atomic object. STND.CXX.24 covers the unreachable marker of an edition that has one and the builtins of a compiler. STND.CXX.25, STND.CXX.26, and STND.CXX.27 give the warnings, the run-time detection that fails at its first detection, and the library checks in production. STND.CXX.28 to STND.CXX.31 refer to the high-assurance coding standard that a decision record adopts, a term that reads "for C" in its C reading.

Provisions of STND.CXX that do not apply. STND.CXX.1 names the Core Guidelines, which concern C++. STND.CXX.2 carries the custodian's direction for the latest edition of C++, so this standard sets no edition for C, and STND.DEPS.4 reviews the stated one with the pinned revisions; STND.CXX.3 depends on it, and STND.STDC.3 asks the record of a lowering instead. STND.CXX.5 and STND.CXX.20 rest on destructors and resource handles, STND.CXX.6 on view types, STND.CXX.8 and STND.CXX.10 on reasons specific to the C++ library and headers, STND.CXX.11 and STND.CXX.13 on templates, and STND.CXX.19 and STND.CXX.21 on C++ exceptions and destructors; STND.STDC.5, STND.STDC.7, STND.STDC.8, STND.STDC.29, STND.STDC.30, and STND.STDC.31 state their outcomes for C. The naming table of STND.CXX.14 is the custodian's convention for C++, and names in C follow STND.DOCS.12 and the layout tool of STND.CODE.5. STND.CXX.22 binds the C++ side of a boundary; c1 leaves unverified that the ISO C standard defines a nonlocal jump only within C frames, so no provision binds the C side, and STND.OMP.21 keeps such a jump out of a structured block of OpenMP.

What the corpus already holds. STND.CODE.3 to STND.CODE.7, STND.CODE.11 to STND.CODE.13 apply to C as to every language: contracts at declarations, static checks with a suppression and its reason at the location of each finding left in place, a layout tool, unremovable checks of untrusted input, no discarded failure of a kind that PRIME.4 lists, no code disabled in comments, a reason for each other discard, and statements grouped by step. A block under a condition that is always false is disabled code in the sense of STND.CODE.11, and STND.STDC.25 does not count it as a branch to compile. STND.CONC applies to C by its own scope, a thread and a signal handler being concurrent tasks: protection of shared objects (STND.CONC.3), sequentially consistent atomics or a reason (STND.CONC.4), lifetimes (STND.CONC.5), concurrent calls in contracts (STND.CONC.6), lock order and conduct, the release of a lock on every path among them (STND.CONC.7, STND.CONC.8), joined threads (STND.CONC.12), signal handling left unchanged by libraries (STND.CONC.17), child processes (STND.CONC.19), and race detection (STND.CONC.25). STND.DEPS.8 records each compiler revision, STND.DEPS.9 each analyzer, and STND.DEPS.11 keeps a copied library apart, which is why the term C source leaves it out. NUMER.16 and NUMER.17 govern options that relax floating-point semantics, NUMER.5 and NUMER.16 a narrowing of a floating-point value, HW.5 the delivered arithmetic, and HW.CPU.3 the floating-point control state, which C changes through its floating-point environment; whether such code also needs the pragma for access to that environment is unverified.

Ownership without destructors. C has no destructor, so an owned resource is released by code that each path out of a function reaches (c1, Resource management without destructors). One kernel coding style gives the reasons for one exit region per function, reached by jumps to labels that release in reverse order: the code is easier to follow, nesting is reduced, and no change updates one exit point and misses another (c1, S15). A wrapper that owns the release is the other form that STND.STDC.5 admits. A cleanup attribute of some compilers and a deferred-release facility on the agenda of the committee of the language are not part of a published edition (unverified). A reallocation whose result overwrites the only pointer to the block loses the block on failure, a path on which it is not released (STND.STDC.5), and a size computed as a count times an element size wraps (STND.STDC.18; c1, S21). An allocation result used without a test discards a listed failure, which STND.CODE.7 forbids.

Undefined behavior. The overview annex of the working draft of the newest edition lists 221 circumstances of undefined behavior (c1, S1; unverified against the published text). A compiler is allowed to assume that none occurs, so an optimizer removes code that is correct only if one does: a test that x + 1 exceeds x becomes true, and a dereference ahead of a test for null lets the test go (c1, S4). A check that relies on the undefined operation itself is removed, a pointer plus a length compared with the pointer among them (c1, S6; STND.STDC.13); the same study found 160 confirmed bugs of code that an optimizer is able to discard, and estimated that such code exists in 40 percent of 8,575 packages of one distribution. STND.STDC.8 to STND.STDC.15 cover the classes that c1 finds behind most defects. Signed overflow stayed undefined when two's complement became the only representation (c1, hazard table). Compilers treat reads of uninitialized objects as license (c1, hazard table), and an option that initializes automatic storage is a defense beside STND.STDC.10, not a replacement. Padding written out of the process discloses earlier memory and makes two runs write different bytes (REPRO.3). A survey of 323 people who write C found that practice, compilers, and the standard disagree on provenance, uninitialized values, padding, and effective types (c1, S11), so STND.STDC.14 states what the standard defines. One kernel builds with the aliasing assumption off, signed overflow wrapping, null-check removal off, and automatic variables initialized (c1, S3). The C reading of STND.CXX.4 states such an option, and STND.STDC.16 keeps correctness from resting on it; its second sentence runs the tests in a build named apart under STND.BUILD.13 without the option, and the build that detects undefined behavior under STND.CXX.26 is a natural choice, since its detector reports what the option hides. Intended unsigned wraparound, in a hash among them, is no defect, and a detector's report of it is suppressed at its location (STND.CODE.4).

Showing an operation safe. STND.STDC.8, STND.STDC.11, and STND.STDC.18 admit a check before the operation, a static check, a stated argument, and for arithmetic a checked operation, which the newest edition provides (c1, S1). STND.STDC.11 reaches operands from outside the function (c1, practice 2). An index from untrusted input is checked where it enters, as DSGN.2 and STND.STDC.21 ask, and a comment is no substitute there. C promotes integer types narrower than int and converts mixed signedness to unsigned, so a narrow unsigned operand is shifted as a signed int and a negative value compared with an unsigned one becomes large (c1, Integer conversions and promotions); STND.STDC.12 therefore judges a left shift by the promoted type. Intended and unintended, defined and undefined overflow are all common (c1, S5), so a check of integer arithmetic is not a report of every overflow.

Strings, buffers, and macros. A C string has no length, so each operation trusts its termination or carries a size (c1, Strings and buffers). One kernel project removed or replaced the copy functions that fail to terminate, take no bound, or read past an unterminated source, and the optional bounds-checking interfaces of the language were found intrusive and were not shipped by the common library (c1, S21 and S37), so STND.STDC.19 to STND.STDC.21 state the outcome and leave the means to the project. STND.STDC.20 concerns strings and bytes; a narrowing of a numerical value is NUMER's. Compilers diagnose a format string that does not match its arguments (c1, S19). A macro that evaluates an argument twice repeats its side effect, an unparenthesized parameter binds to the operators around it, and a branch that no configuration builds has had no check (c1, The preprocessor); later editions moved constant objects, static assertions, and type inference into the language (STND.STDC.23). A result computed on a branch recorded as unchecked has no evidence from the checks (PRIME.3).

Restrict and variable-length arrays. A violation of a restrict qualifier is undefined, and removing the qualifier from a conforming program leaves its meaning unchanged, so a build without it is a reference for a test (c1, practice 17; STND.STDC.26). That build is able to order floating-point operations differently, so the comparison agrees within the tolerance of the acceptance criterion, bitwise only where the criterion asks it (STND.VVUQ.82), on the basis that NUMER.11 gives. Variable-length array objects are a conditional feature, and one kernel project removed them from its stack, citing worse machine code and a stack that grows past its segment (c1, S1 and S21); STND.STDC.27 bounds a size from untrusted input, and STND.STDC.4 tests for the feature.

Failures. PRIME.4 lists invalid input, detected precondition violations, non-convergence, and resource exhaustion, and its last sentence excludes silent continuation past an error. The C library reports failure by a return value, by errno, by the error indicator of a stream, or not at all (c1, Error reporting and ignored returns). The working draft states that errno is zero at startup, that a library function is able to set it whether or not an error occurred unless its documentation states otherwise, and that no library function is required to set it to zero (c1, S1, 7.5), from which STND.STDC.32 follows; a separate errno for each thread is unverified. A condition in errno alone is lost at the next call that sets errno and escapes the diagnosis of STND.CXX.17, which the row of STND.CXX.16 excludes.

Streams. That a failed write sets the error indicator of its stream until code clears it, and that a flush or a close writes buffered data and reports a failure of that write, are not in c1 and are unverified. STND.STDC.28 asks, as a default, the tests that receive those failures and an answer to each, for every stream whose output a result relies on, closed or only flushed. A full volume is resource exhaustion, which PRIME.4 lists; another output error is not in its list, and a result reported after failed writes continues past an error, which the last sentence of PRIME.4 excludes. STND.STDC.28 stays a default because the facts on which a binding form would rest, the error indicator and the report of a flush, are unverified, and no binding provision rests on an unverified fact. Under STND.STDC.28, a formatted write to a tested stream is not tested by itself, and STND.STDC.29 has such a call carry the suppression of STND.CODE.4 that the warning of the C reading of STND.CXX.25 accepts; a call whose failure an error handler that ends the run receives, as STND.MPI.5 admits, carries the same. STND.STDC.30 covers the release of other resources.

Ignored results. The rule on errors of the standard library in the CERT C coding standard lists functions whose results code is let ignore, among them formatted output to the standard output, the copying and filling functions of memory, and writes to the standard output and standard error discarded by a cast to void (c1, S16). For a failure of a kind that PRIME.4 lists, STND.CODE.7 prevails, because a coding standard is adopted under a default (STND.CXX.28) and a binding provision is only tightened below it (GOV.19). For another failure, an ignored result carries its reason under STND.CODE.12, so an entry of that list is a case for STND.CODE.12 and not an exemption. A function whose result reports no failure is outside both. A compiler or analyzer that knows the library functions meets STND.STDC.29 without a wrapper. STND.STDC.29 and the second sentence of STND.STDC.22 ask their diagnosis where the means of the work include one, so that a toolchain without the attribute or the check blocks no work, and STND.CODE.7 still forbids discarding a listed failure.

Signals and concurrency. A signal handler is allowed to refer to an object of static or thread storage duration only where it is a lock-free atomic object or is assigned as volatile sig_atomic_t, and to call only a short list of library functions, and a handler that returns after a signal of a computational exception is undefined (c1, S1, 7.14.1.1). A platform lists more functions that are safe in a handler, says that the standard input and output functions are unsafe because they share buffers, and has a handler save and restore errno (c1, S22 and S23). The object rule is the C reading of STND.CXX.23, whose condition also reaches an interrupt handler of a constrained target; STND.STDC.33 and STND.STDC.34 add the calls, errno, and the return. The edition that added threads defined a memory model under which a data race is undefined and plain operations on atomic objects are sequentially consistent (c1, S1), so STND.CONC.3 and STND.CONC.4 apply as written and a volatile qualifier is not a protection. Adjacent bit-fields share one memory location (c1, S1), which STND.STDC.35 has one protection cover, and HW.11 verifies the protocol under that model. Whether a mutex of the C library works with a condition variable of the platform library, and whether consume ordering is used in practice, are unverified; STND.CONC.3 and STND.CONC.4 govern both.

Interfaces to other languages. C is the common binary interface of most languages on a platform (c1, Interfaces to other languages). A Fortran caller sees the dimensions of an array reversed, indexes from one, and has no unsigned types (c1, S26), which STND.STDC.36 has the contract state; a Python extension holds counted references, new or borrowed, whose owner and period of validity the C reading of STND.CXX.15 states. That no jump crosses a frame of another language rests on a basis that c1 leaves unverified and is not a provision.

Checking. Run-time detectors of invalid accesses run at about twice the time and are documented as tools for finding bugs, and the detector of undefined behavior also reports defined unsigned overflow and implicit conversion (c1, S30 and S31). The second sentence of STND.STDC.37 lists the kinds that STND.STDC.8, STND.STDC.9, STND.STDC.10, STND.STDC.11, STND.STDC.12, STND.STDC.14, and STND.STDC.27 govern; STND.STDC.13 concerns how a check is written and adds none. One compiler's overflow warning has done nothing for years (c1, S41), so the first sentence names diagnostics by what they detect. A compiler's analyzer for C is documented as neither sound nor complete (c1, S32), and a deductive verifier proves contracts (c1, S33); STND.STDC.38 has the record say which a verification is, and for data races one record meets it and STND.CONC.27.

High assurance. The two coding standards for C in c1 are suited to adoption under the C reading of STND.CXX.28, and the published coverage of one ends below the newest edition and of the other at an older one (c1, S28, S29, and S40), so the C reading of STND.CXX.30 fixes the edition of high-assurance code at a covered one unless a decision record admits a feature of a later edition. Conformance reduces undefined behavior by a mechanical check and is not evidence that a result is correct (VV.1).

Floor. STND.STDC.2 is binding and rests on PRIME.4 and PRIME.2 alone. The C reading of STND.CXX.16 admits only the responses of PRIME.4 and the return of the condition to a caller that responds; its row's exclusion of errno alone is text new to this standard, without which the condition is lost at the next call. The C reading of STND.CXX.17 asks one attribute per function where the edition or the toolchain provides one, and otherwise a static check that diagnoses the ignored result; without either, a returned listed failure is ignored silently. Its row has it apply where the means of the work include such a compiler or static check, so that a toolchain without either blocks no work, and STND.CODE.7 still forbids discarding a listed failure there. The C reading of STND.CXX.18 keeps in every build a check that CPS.5, CPS.9, or STND.CODE.6 requires, at the cost of one choice of option. The C reading of STND.CXX.29 asks a claim of conformance to name the means that checked it, which PRIME.2 asks of every claim. None asks for work that PRIME.4 or PRIME.2 does not, so none raises the floor (GOV.11). The rulings that STND.CXX.16, STND.CXX.17, STND.CXX.18, and STND.CXX.29 raise no floor were made for C++; the argument for C, the errno clause and the static check where no attribute exists included, is the one above. STND.STDC.2 binds at every class; the C reading of STND.CXX.29 reaches only high-assurance code, never below CSQ1.

Class onset. Under STND.CLASS.10, each default instantiates only DSGN, SIMPL, INTPR, REPRO, RSRC, VV, or a provision of HW, and applies from CSQ3; work of CSQ4 may omit it. The part of a default that rests on a row of that table applies from the class of the row, and the rows of STND.STDC.1 apply from the classes that its table gives. The row of DSGN.2 is read as STND.CXX reads it: a check of untrusted input where it enters applies from CSQ3, with DSGN.2 itself, and the retained checks of other preconditions, of postconditions, and of invariants, among them those of STND.CXX.24 and STND.CXX.27, from CSQ1. STND.STDC.21, STND.STDC.22, and STND.STDC.27 check untrusted input where it enters and apply from CSQ3. STND.STDC.8, STND.STDC.11, STND.STDC.12, STND.STDC.13, and STND.STDC.18 ask that an operation be shown safe by any of their means, the obligation of DSGN.1, and apply from CSQ3. STND.STDC.7 and STND.STDC.36 rest on DSGN.4 and the first sentence of STND.STDC.29 on DSGN.5, and apply from CSQ2, as STND.CXX.6 and STND.CXX.21 do; the second sentence of STND.STDC.29 rests on REPRO.7 and STND.CODE.4 and applies from CSQ3. STND.STDC.38 rests on INTPR.10 and applies from CSQ2, as STND.CONC.27 does. STND.STDC.33 and STND.STDC.35 state what a protocol uses under DSGN.15, not how its invariants are verified, and apply from CSQ3. The tests of STND.STDC.16 and STND.STDC.26 compare with a build of the same code, not with a reference oracle produced without it, and apply from CSQ3. STND.STDC.3, STND.STDC.4, the second sentence of STND.STDC.22, STND.STDC.23, STND.STDC.25, and STND.STDC.37 instantiate DSGN.3, rest on no row, and apply from CSQ3; the mechanical checking of each contract of a safety-related component, which STND.CLASS.10 starts at CSQ0, stays with that row. STND.STDC.28 and the other defaults rest on no row and apply from CSQ3. No default raises the floor (GOV.11).

Existing C source conforms to the default provisions when a change next touches it."""

